<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day26" xml:id="day-26-lecture">
    <title>Day 26</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            9.3: Support Vector Machines (SVMs)
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
    
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>How is nonlinearity introduced in Support Vector Machines?
      <ul>
        <li><p>You should be able to describe how kernels work conceptually and mathematically.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>What type of kernel makes a Support Vector Machine equivalent to a Support Vector Classifier?</p></li>
    <li><p>What other kernels are there?</p></li>
    <li><p>What type of kernel leads to more local behavior?</p></li>
    <li>
      <p>How do you use Support Vector Machines in Python?
      <ul>
        <li><p>You should be able to fit a Support Vector Machine using different kernels covered in class.</p></li>
        <li><p>You should be able to select the appropriate hyperparameter that optimizes the bias-variance trade-off.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
        
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Enlarging the feature space</title>

        <image source="figures/enlarging_feature_space" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>Non-linearly separable data drawn on the plane of predictors X1 and X2 on the left panel, and the same data linearly separable by a hyperplane when enlarging the feature space to the sum of the squared predictors.</shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
            Problem: Data not linearly separable. What if we enlarge the feature space?
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Enlarge using <m>Z=X^2 + Y^2</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The data becomes linearly separable in the enlarged space!
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        

    <p>
  Say we want to enlarge the feature space using <m>2p</m> features:

<md>X_1, X_1^2, X_2, X_2^2, \dots, X_p, X_p^2</md>
</p>

<p>
  Optimization becomes:
</p>

<p>
<md>
  <mrow>\amp \underset{\beta_0, \beta_{11}, \beta_{12}, \dots, \beta_{p1}, \beta_{p2}, \epsilon_1, \dots, \epsilon_n, M}{\text{maximize}} \quad M</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp \text{subject to } y_i \left( \beta_0 + \sum_{j=1}^p \beta_{j1}x_{ij} + \sum_{j=1}^p \beta_{j2}x_{ij}^2 \right) \geq M(1 - \epsilon_i),</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp \sum_{i=1}^n \epsilon_i \leq C, \quad \epsilon_i \geq 0, \quad \sum_{j=1}^p \sum_{k=1}^2 \beta_{jk}^2 = 1.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
    This becomes unwieldly if we have to check, say every degree 2 monomial <m>X_iX_j</m>, so need something more efficient.         
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        What if we want to enlarge the feature space using non-polynomial functions. How do we do that?
      </p>
    </li>
    </ul>
    </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
  <title>Inner products as a measure of similarity</title>
  <p>
    <ul>
<li>
  <p>
    The support vector machine allows enlarging the feature space in a computationally efficient way.
  </p>
</li>
  </ul>
</p>

    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{x}=(x_1,\ldots, x_p)</m> be a new test observation. The SVC can be written as
      <md>
      <mrow>f(x) \amp= \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \ldots + \beta_p x_p</mrow>
      <mrow>     \amp= \beta_0 + \sum\limits_{j=1}^p{\beta_j x_j}</mrow>
      <mrow>     \amp= \beta_0 +  [x_1,\ldots,x_p]\cdot [\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p]</mrow>
      <mrow>     \amp= \beta_0 + \langle\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\beta} \rangle </mrow>
      </md>      
    </p>

    <p>
      The same SVC can be equivalently written using observation weights <m>\alpha_i</m> according to
      <md>
      <mrow>f(x) \amp= \beta_0 + (\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{i1}}) x_1 + (\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{i2}}) x_2 + \ldots + (\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{ip}}) x_p</mrow>
        <mrow>   \amp= \beta_0 + \sum\limits_{j=1}^p{(\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{ij}}) x_j} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \beta_0 + \sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i \langle x, x_i\rangle}</mrow>
      </md>
      Only support vectors have <m>\alpha_i \neq 0.</m> So if we let the <m>\mathcal{S}</m> be the set of indices for the support vectors among the <m>n</m> data points, then we only need to evaluate <m>\mathcal{S}</m> terms
      <md>f(x)=\beta_0 + \sum\limits_{i \in \mathcal{S}}{\alpha_i \langle x,x_i \rangle}.</md>   
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            To fit <m>\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n</m> and <m>\beta_0</m>, we need the <m>\binom{n}{2}</m> inner products <m>\langle x_i, x_i' \rangle</m> between all pairs of training observations.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The inner product is considered a measure of similarity.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>      
    </p>
  


  <definition xml:id="def-dot-product">
  <title>Dot/Inner product</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For vectors in a Euclidean space, the dot product is defined as
        <md>
  \vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = \begin{cases}

    |\vec{a}| |\vec{b}| \cos{\gamma}, &amp; \text{if } \vec{a} \neq \vec{0} \text{ and } \vec{b} \neq \vec{0}, \\
    0, &amp; \text{if } \vec{a} = \vec{0} \text{ or } \vec{b} = \vec{0}.
  \end{cases}
</md>
where <m>0 \leq \gamma \leq \pi</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="figures/dotprod" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>Three panels showing two vectors with an angle between them that is greater than, equal, and less than zero, respectively.</shortdescription>        
      </image>
      <p>
        More generally, we can define an inner product between two vectors <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> from a vector space (not generally Euclidean), and we denote the corresponding inner product by 
        <md>
          \langle \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b} \rangle.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>  
</paragraphs>
<example>
  <title>Dot product and similarity</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Given the shown vectors, Find the dot product in each case. How is the similarity of the vectors quantified using the dot product in each case?
    </p>
    <image source="figures/dot_product_four_examples" width="70%">
      <shortdescription>Four panels, each showing two vectors. The first three panels show vectors in the plane, while the third panel shows a vector in 3D.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Figure (a).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{a}\cdot \mathbf{b}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. The vectors point in the same direction (similar). 
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Figure (b).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{a}\cdot \mathbf{b}=0</m>. The two vectors are unrelated (perpendicular). 
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Figure (c).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{a}\cdot \mathbf{b}=-1</m>. The two vectors point in opposite directions (dissimilar).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Figure (d).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{a}\cdot \mathbf{b}=5+0+6=11</m>. The vectors point in the same direction (similar). 
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>  
</example>

<exercise>
  <title>More dot products practice</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Compute the following dot products.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(1,1), (0,3) \rangle</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(1,1), (0,3) \rangle=0+3=3.</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
    <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(1,1), (3,2) \rangle</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(1,1), (3,2) \rangle=3+2=5.</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
    <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(2,3), (0,3) \rangle</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(2,3), (0,3) \rangle=0+9=9.</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
    <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(2,3), (3,2) \rangle</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\langle(2,3), (3,2) \rangle=6+6=12.</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>  
  </exercise>


  <paragraphs>
    <title>Enlarging the feature space via kernels</title>
    <p>Recall that the support vector classifier can be written as
      <md>f(x)=\beta_0 + \sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i \langle x, x_i\rangle}.</md>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            So we only need to know how close or similar the data points are to each other (not there exact coordinates in the enlarged space). 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Computing the similarity between data points turns out to be way more efficient than evaluating their absolute coordinates in the enlarged space.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>kernel</term> <m>K(x_i,x_i')</m> is a more generalized way of thinking about how close or similar to samples are. It provides a family of alternatives and generalizations to the dot product <m>\langle x_i,x_i' \rangle</m>. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Using the language of kernels, <m>\langle x_i,x_i' \rangle</m> is known as the <term>linear kernel</term>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p> 
<p>
    We can now write our <term>support vector machine</term> (SVM) as
<md>
  <mrow>y_i f(x_i) \geq M(1 - \epsilon_i), \text{ where}</mrow>
  <mrow>f(x) = \beta_0 + \sum_{i \in \mathcal{S}} \alpha_i K(x, x_i).</mrow>
</md>
<ul>
  <li>
    <p>
      SVM is what we get when we combine a non-linear kernel <m>K</m> with an SVC classifier.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      We choose <m>K</m> in advance.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
    
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>A polynomial kernel</title>
  <p>
    <md>
      K(x_i,x_{i'})=
      \left(1+\sum_{j=1}^{p}x_{ij}x_{i'j}\right)^d
    </md>
  </p>
  <sidebyside widths="55% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul >
          <li><p>Much more flexible decision boundary.</p></li>
          <li><p>Amounts to fitting a support vector classifier in a higher-dimensional space involving polynomials of degree <m>d</m>, rather than in the original feature space.</p></li>
          <li><p>Right: degree 3 polynomial kernel.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <image source="figures/Fig9-9a.png">
      <shortdescription>Example of a support vector classifier using a polynomial kernel.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>A radial kernel</title>
  <p>
    <md>
      K(x_i,x_{i'})=
      \exp\left(-\gamma\sum_{j=1}^{p}(x_{ij}-x_{i'j})^2\right)
    </md>
  </p>
  <sidebyside widths="55% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul >
          <li><p>This is essentially a Gaussian kernel.</p></li>
          <li><p>A large distance makes <m>\sum_j(x_{ij}-x_{i'j})^2</m> large, but then <m>e^{-\text{large}}</m> is tiny.</p></li>
          <li><p>In <m>f(x)</m>, this means that <m>x_i</m> plays little role if it is far away.</p></li>
          <li><p>The radial kernel has local behavior.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <image source="figures/Fig9-9b.png">
      <shortdescription>Example of a support vector classifier using a radial kernel.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>One-Vs-One Classification</title>
  <p>Also called all-pairs.</p>


      <algorithm xml:id="one-vs-one-svm">
  <title>One-Vs-One Classification</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Given a new observation <m>x^*</m> and <m>K&gt;2</m> possible classes:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Construct <m>\binom{K}{2}</m> binary SVM classifiers, one for each pair of classes.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For each pair of classes, use the corresponding SVM to classify the observation <m>x^*</m> as belonging to one of the two classes. The predicted class receives one vote.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Assign the observation to the class that receives the most votes across all pairwise classifiers.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</algorithm>

<example>
  <title>One-Vs-One Classification</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Assume we have <m>K</m> classes:
      <m>\{\text{apple},\text{banana},\text{orange},\text{kiwi}\}</m>. Given a test observation <m>x^*</m>, how do we use SVM to classify it?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
        <p>For one-vs-one classification, we fit <m>\binom{K}{2}</m> SVMs, one for each pair of classes:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Apple vs. banana</p></li>
        <li><p>Apple vs. orange</p></li>
        <li><p>Apple vs. kiwi</p></li>
        <li><p>Banana vs. orange</p></li>
        <li><p>Banana vs. kiwi</p></li>
        <li><p>Orange vs. kiwi</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>Given a test observation <m>x^*</m>, assign <m>x^*</m> to the class that is most frequently predicted by the pairwise classifiers (i.e., the class that receives the most votes).</p>
  </solution>
</example>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>One-Vs-All Classification</title>

      <algorithm xml:id="one-vs-all-svm">
  <title>One-Vs-All Classification</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Given a new observation <m>x^*</m> and <m>K&gt;2</m> possible classes:</p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Fit <m>K</m> binary SVM classifiers, each comparing one class (coded as +1) against the remaining <m>K-1</m> classes (coded as -1).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>For each class <m>k</m>, determine the fitted coefficients and evaluate the decision function at <m>x^*</m>:</p>
          <p>
            <md>
              f_k(x^*)=\beta_{0k}+\beta_{1k}x_1^*
              +\cdots+\beta_{pk}x_p^*.
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Assign the observation to the class <m>k</m> for which <m>f_k(x^*)</m> is largest. This amounts to a high level of confidence that the test observation belongs to the kth class rather than to any of the other classes.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</algorithm>

<example>
  <title>One-Vs-All Classification</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Suppose we want to classify fruit into one of four classes:
      <m>\{\text{apples},\text{bananas},\text{oranges},\text{strawberries}\}</m>. How can we can we classify a new observation <m>x^*</m> using one-vs-all classification?
    </p>
    
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>For one-vs-all classification, we fit <m>K=4</m> SVMs, where each SVM distinguishes one class from the remaining three classes:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Apples vs. not apples (bananas, oranges, strawberries)</p></li>
        <li><p>Bananas vs. not banana (apples, oranges, strawberries)</p></li>
        <li><p>Oranges vs. not oranges (apples, bananas, strawberries)</p></li>
        <li><p>Strawberries vs. not strawberries (apples, bananas, oranges)</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>Given a test observation <m>x^*</m>, evaluate the four fitted decision functions:</p>
    <p>
      <md>
        \begin{aligned}
        f_1(x^*)&amp;=\beta_{01}+\beta_{11}x_1^*+\cdots+\beta_{p1}x_p^*,\\
        f_2(x^*)&amp;=\beta_{02}+\beta_{12}x_1^*+\cdots+\beta_{p2}x_p^*,\\
        f_3(x^*)&amp;=\beta_{03}+\beta_{13}x_1^*+\cdots+\beta_{p3}x_p^*,\\
        f_4(x^*)&amp;=\beta_{04}+\beta_{14}x_1^*+\cdots+\beta_{p4}x_p^*.
        \end{aligned}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>Assign the test observation to the class corresponding to the largest decision-function value.
    
      <!-- <md>
        \widehat{y}(x^*)=\underset{k\in\{1,2,3,4\}}{\operatorname{argmax}}\ f_k(x^*).
      </md> -->
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

</paragraphs>
<paragraphs>
  <title>TL;DR</title>

      <p>SVM:
        <md>
          f(x)=\beta_0+\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}\alpha_i K(x,x_i)
        </md>
      </p>

      <p><m>\text{Kernels}</m></p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Linear</p>
            <p>
              <md>
                K(x_i,x_{i'})=\sum_{j=1}^{p}x_{ij}x_{i'j}
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Polynomial</p>
            <p>
              <md>
                K(x_i,x_{i'})=
                \left(1+\sum_{j=1}^{p}x_{ij}x_{i'j}\right)^d
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Radial</p>
            <p>
              <md>
                K(x_i,x_{i'})=
                \exp\left(-\gamma\sum_{j=1}^{p}
                (x_{ij}-x_{i'j})^2\right)
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

</paragraphs>



</handout>